#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134632} :activity/id #uuid "02348ec0-92a1-55fa-a513-9148a7a85088" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169 :topic/id #uuid "8546fff0-2cdf-5662-94b8-db689cb12a4a" :topic/math-academy-id 2096} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135033 :tutorial/content "Let $V$ be a vector space. Any function $f:V \\times V \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that takes two vectors $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\in V$ and returns a real number is an **inner product** on $V,$ denoted $\\langle \\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\rangle,$ if the following axioms are satisfied:\n\n- Linearity in the first argument:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩ \\\\  & ⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩,\\,\\alpha \\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\rangle = \\langle \\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle\n$$\n\n- Positive definiteness:\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle \\gt 0\\quad \\textrm{for}\\quad  \\mathbf a\\neq  \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{a} \\rangle = 0\\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nThe concept of an inner product is a generalization of the dot product. It allows us to apply the geometric ideas of length and angle to abstract vector spaces. \n\nNotice that the standard dot product on $\\mathbb{R}^n$ indeed satisfies these axioms. Recall that if $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf v \\in \\mathbb R^n,$ then\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf u, \\mathbf v\\rangle = \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = u_1\\cdot v_1 + u_2\\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n\\cdot v_n .\n$$\n\nLet $\\mathbf u, \\mathbf v, \\mathbf w \\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb R.$ Let's now check that our three axioms are satisfied. \n\n- We first show that the dot product is linear in the first argument. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u+v,w⟩ & =(u+v)\\cdot w \\\\  & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =⟨u,w⟩+⟨v,w⟩\\,✓ \\\\ ⟨\\alpha u,v⟩ & =(\\alpha u)\\cdot v \\\\  & =(\\alpha u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\alpha (u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\alpha ⟨u,v⟩\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we show that the dot product is symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u,v⟩ & =u\\cdot v \\\\  & =v\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨v,u⟩\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we show that the dot product is positive definite:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨u,u⟩ & =u\\cdot u \\\\  & ={u}_{1}{u}_{1}+{u}_{2}{u}_{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}{u}_{n} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{u}_{n}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is clearly positive for all $\\mathbf u\\neq\\mathbf 0$ and is zero for $\\mathbf u = \\mathbf 0 \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nSince the dot product satisfies our three axioms, we conclude that it is an inner product. \n\nFinally, note that since an inner product is both linear in the first argument and symmetric, then it is also linear in the second argument." :tutorial/id #uuid "5640068c-e4b6-5268-9c3b-d2c6f0906725" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3604 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "30a6ea64-4bb3-5509-b69d-433ee390d90f" :step/math-academy-id 20189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133571 :question/id #uuid "d8ab385f-ca7e-5ac2-9c0c-23ec78c9b1f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e46fa568-0a40-5b36-a01f-20555db9b055" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049814}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444597}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "2cb651dc-e28c-599d-bca7-600e9e22174f" :step/math-academy-id 20190 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134748}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133763 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133572 :question/id #uuid "d7217dd1-5e4d-5ecb-96a4-e4c7064f2f88" :question/math-academy-id "e-5543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efed2bff-625e-556d-99a3-9b51f9d68937" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050396}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512906}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "bb70017f-2feb-562e-9c18-92b37a4a40ee" :step/math-academy-id 20191 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133764 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133573 :question/id #uuid "007b171e-6587-5835-a59c-443b29c2b667" :question/math-academy-id "e-5544"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c553cb8-9f5c-5fa5-bc43-db7d7b64755f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512865}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Continuous Functions"} :step/id #uuid "6655ce76-39d7-5522-a425-3a237edac607" :step/math-academy-id 20192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135077 :tutorial/content "The first type of inner product that we will discuss is the **weighted dot product**. \n\nGiven a sequence of positive real numbers $d_1, d_2, \\ldots d_n > 0,$ called \"weights\", the function\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = \\sum_{i=1}^n d_i x_i y_i\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is similar to the dot product, except here, different components are given different weights." :tutorial/id #uuid "97a9d9a1-c4ef-55fb-ab09-32189a73c2ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3992 :tutorial/title "The Weighted Dot Product"} :step/id #uuid "55c93c2b-8932-5053-b4c4-297bb2faf2ce" :step/math-academy-id 23793 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134633}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134748 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135078 :tutorial/content "The next inner product that we will discuss concerns inner products on polynomials.\n\nGiven a sequence of distinct real numbers ![](images/52/52cae406fa2952a80a1bab7e02d5667b3acda56e4b2456a3dcb6600a91105482.svg) the function\n![](images/d1/d1f9dd085bdf1f3d49f99a26d17d75a4e1bd1ee413fff06bfff4198e4e1ee59d.svg)\ndefines an inner product on ![](images/35/353273654347dcd414aa60782023eff4d676ea7f598a8d2f9c923ee6c4cba811.svg) the vector space of polynomials of degree at most ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg)\n\nLet's consider a few examples:\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/ba/ba47d908e29aa7475933ff0536d610abe1e3752dd165c8cf6f655f83ae48156b.svg)\n![](images/24/24d15f48f890f3bff207460d175db7d16b35fd21a05e1dc3d1103cde1453c531.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/50/507c62e239664ab41c74f85c0e905c0a84d3da5183991232db789ce8a9d385f1.svg)\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/c5/c5c6bb73a744c88af37d2cbce6ac9e27957fc77dbcddb4ec6fb04fb02ac1bbc2.svg)\n![](images/5f/5f66a28d825609fe3e9c6dca065cb3f96e6fe0fafccd56ff87d7608fa7cf35af.svg)\n\nNotice that this inner product on ![](images/29/297749fafabb3d9d253c58491c364d113f5579eacda31a07a8b8c792cc9d7777.svg) requires summing ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) terms. This is essential to ensure positive definiteness. For example, the function\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\nis *not* an inner product on ![](images/68/686b2d83c1abe018b786ecc5f7c624a4932fda35b57d09ca5385a41f8a3bb233.svg) since there exist nonzero polynomials ![](images/aa/aa374f18ad64a3743718af3bbcef010a756b25dae566ccb9413a790a97b72067.svg) such that ![](images/d1/d138796b5b181d78264c70ef5c9b68db4dba9c6603612cca303c87e09924ec2d.svg) which violates the condition ![](images/63/631ddd9e222592497d44379cd2d6b6aac118ff1a0e4c6587347473112aae26c5.svg)\n\nIn general, a nonzero polynomial of degree at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) can have at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) distinct roots, so vanishing at ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) distinct points forces the polynomial to be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "e88c3857-ea1b-58ee-8584-5e787b53f7f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3993 :tutorial/title "An Inner Product of Polynomials in One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "71837f9d-0b3c-5b65-af89-30a509dbded1" :step/math-academy-id 23794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135079 :tutorial/content "Our final inner product is a generalization of what we saw in the previous example. The function\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{a}^{b} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathcal{C}[a,b],$ the vector space of continuous functions on $[a,b].$ \n\nHere, instead of finding the product of the functions at some discrete points and summing them, we sum (integrate) the product of the functions over the whole interval $[a,b].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9d7e7a9f-6893-5af3-b47e-e5ef2ccba9f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3994 :tutorial/title "An Inner Product of Functions in One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "db12ef78-1aac-5236-9cfa-e2a0ed77fabc" :step/math-academy-id 23795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134635}}] :activity/title "Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133983}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512996} :activity/id #uuid "e9da63ab-03d4-5b28-8d6e-c384781de626" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169 :topic/id #uuid "8546fff0-2cdf-5662-94b8-db689cb12a4a" :topic/math-academy-id 2096} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21b9f9b0-0c28-514b-949a-8339e5b0f60e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512171} {:answer/id #uuid "338b9ad0-884f-5a4e-8674-e7a8e1019500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512211} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4a6c4d-c28d-5c73-8281-c978daf12c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512393} {:answer/id #uuid "c61412bc-118a-5e93-b6c0-91732aceaf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512576} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b7320f-6d58-56c5-83c3-fa0d4c1b8eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512211} :answer-field/id #uuid "a756d2bc-643b-5bbc-b0e1-1d9618cee002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34" :question/math-academy-id "q-76450" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3dbec995-d7b1-5d9d-9fb5-ba6abfa94b3e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44900815-93d0-5419-bda4-15f71f1c2ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327533} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a67c3-6516-5ef0-8910-205fb5e42518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328165} {:answer/id #uuid "919c9679-b1d6-53d8-ba04-8bb16361e1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}-t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328202} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bf4da2-ee9f-5281-99ba-7b938e201031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328542} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bd2329-945b-550c-9f1d-b21cc4aa33ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328767} :answer-field/id #uuid "914fc28e-fc9f-53b3-abf8-9222f8795d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71904bfc-c560-50f5-ac2b-41eb0a528292" :question/math-academy-id "q-42256" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2].$ Find $⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "512ebfd7-20c8-529c-8047-68f33115cdf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d9ec8b-a60d-5b77-ac5a-1965ca2efb92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512212} {:answer/id #uuid "34ee9bea-a7ee-5f69-b60d-dab66dd19aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512217} {:answer/id #uuid "adf80400-9bf0-5756-9a8b-0e7694b4521f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512508} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ec2195-abc8-59fa-8eec-d947e7a76b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512536} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe9ee0f-144d-585c-9fe5-c21ac8f7efcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512217} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ffd9e21-a21c-5ae7-a7c7-e2f36c42374e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768" :question/math-academy-id "q-76442" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+0+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "63e80d05-6040-5f25-99b4-9ebfe7b7e44a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ba600e-0358-513c-9008-d4164bd322af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327215} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5d4c0c-e9e9-5fde-8a0d-8854cd85cbf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328015} {:answer/id #uuid "8e92535a-d563-54ca-91ef-e0c126aef3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328174} {:answer/id #uuid "ac342d67-73ca-57ea-b00b-d2af7ff07ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328429} {:answer/id #uuid "f88f22bf-caeb-53aa-96f2-904a0b368b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328429} :answer-field/id #uuid "76480884-9973-5d4d-b09e-9119b17f3377" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75517719-307f-569b-90da-09de54805f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-42255" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,{cos}^{2}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}]-[-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ecbe002-c0f8-5d49-b600-f72c319b64d5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22514fb0-f6fc-5f81-86f8-4d2e1207589f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512173} {:answer/id #uuid "36b65fb5-0fee-5676-afc4-645c1b803bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512220} {:answer/id #uuid "5261906e-cddb-5dc3-a97d-890d155fa5c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512281} {:answer/id #uuid "db85a92e-4361-5ea7-b58b-801f6714295d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512624} {:answer/id #uuid "e66a7f06-33be-5e84-baf2-c460003b0fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512281} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca6ff97-40bd-5746-ace1-2856a95eb89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" :question/math-academy-id "q-43813" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nFind $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=3+t-2{t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-9+0+3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "93e68841-8807-59e4-aeea-be3ba4a3970a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512993}}] :activity/title "Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512093}]}
